--TEST--
`Core\Debug::dump` writes exactly `render` of each argument plus a newline, so its variadic form, its split form and `render` all agree over one sweep — and `Core\Out::capture` never swallows a dump — spec § 16, `rule:errors/diagnostic-record`, `rule:errors/renderings` and `rule:errors/debug-dump`, `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/inspecting-a-value
// `Core\Debug`'s two members walk one value the same way and differ only in
// where the bytes go (`nvs_stdlib::debug`'s own module doc, § *`render`, and
// why it is one member rather than a second mechanism*). Nothing asserted that
// they *agree*: the rendering case shows what `render` answers row by row, and
// the dump beside it is only asserted to stay off standard output. A `dump`
// that grew its own walk — a shared cycle table across arguments, a container
// laid out differently because it is a record node rather than a root — would
// still print plausibly on its own line.
//
// This is the agreement shape, and the two expectation sections are where it
// is asserted: everything below the "-- the sweep" marker on standard output
// is byte-identical to the whole of standard error, twice over, because the
// same seven subjects go through `render` on one stream and through `dump` on
// the other.
class Sweep {
    // § 3's plaintext view ends a *record* with a newline and a rendered
    // *value* without one, which is the one byte the two members differ by.
    public static function endingInNewline(array<string> $rendered): uint {
        uint $n = 0;
        foreach ($rendered as string $one) {
            if (Core\Str::endsWith($one, "\n")) {
                $n = $n + 1;
            }
        }
        return $n;
    }

    // Counted against the above so the trim is pinned as *trailing only*: a
    // container's rendering is several lines and keeps every newline but its
    // last, which an implementation stripping them all would still pass the
    // count above.
    public static function multiline(array<string> $rendered): uint {
        uint $n = 0;
        foreach ($rendered as string $one) {
            if (Core\Str::contains($one, "\n")) {
                $n = $n + 1;
            }
        }
        return $n;
    }

    // A `bool as string` renders `false` as the empty string, so a yes/no is
    // what makes a lost assertion visible rather than blank.
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        return $b ? "yes" : "no";
    }
}

// The seven subjects, chosen where the two members could part company rather
// than to re-show § 1's node kinds. A control byte (§ 5's substitution, applied
// on the way *into* the record), two container shapes and a nesting level, a
// `secret` property (§ 5's redaction, read off the class), and the same ring
// twice — the last pair being the one that a record-wide cycle table would get
// wrong, since `dump`'s ids are per argument exactly as `render`'s are per
// call.
class Vault {
    public secret string $token = "s3cr3t";
    public int $n = 1;
}
class Ring {
    public lateinit Ring $next;
    public function constructor(public string $name) {}
}
Ring $ring = new Ring("a");
$ring->next = $ring;

string $control = "a\rb";
array<int> $list = [10, 20];
array<int> $map = ["k" => 1];
array<mixed> $nested = [[7]];
Vault $vault = new Vault();

// The renderings, taken once and reused for both the counting and the sweep
// below, so the bytes compared against standard error are the very bytes the
// counts were taken over.
array<string> $rendered = [
    Core\Debug::render($control) as string,
    Core\Debug::render($list) as string,
    Core\Debug::render($map) as string,
    Core\Debug::render($nested) as string,
    Core\Debug::render($vault) as string,
    Core\Debug::render($ring) as string,
    Core\Debug::render($ring) as string,
];

echo "subjects ", Core\Arr::count($rendered), "\n";
echo "ending in a newline ", Sweep::endingInNewline($rendered), "\n";
echo "holding one at all ", Sweep::multiline($rendered), "\n";

// The two renderings of the same ring are the same bytes: the cycle id is
// record-local and restarts per node, so neither member numbers the repeat.
echo "the ring twice ", Sweep::yn($rendered["5"] == $rendered["6"]), "\n";

// § 4, and `nvs_stdlib::debug`'s § *Where a dump lands*: a dump is deliberately
// not captured, because capturing one would swallow the very output it was
// written to make visible. The capture answers what was *echoed* and the dump
// goes past it to the diagnostic channel — the first record on standard error
// below.
var $captured = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "echoed";
    Core\Debug::dump("not swallowed");
});
echo "captured [", $captured, "]\n";
echo "the capture holds the dump ", Sweep::yn(Core\Str::contains($captured as string, "not swallowed")), "\n";

// -- the sweep. From here standard output is `render` per subject plus the one
// newline the record ends with, written twice; standard error is the same
// subjects through the variadic `dump` and then through seven separate ones.
// The two forms are one record each way, so all three renderings agree.
foreach ($rendered as string $one) {
    echo $one, "\n";
}
Core\Debug::dump($control, $list, $map, $nested, $vault, $ring, $ring);

foreach ($rendered as string $one) {
    echo $one, "\n";
}
Core\Debug::dump($control);
Core\Debug::dump($list);
Core\Debug::dump($map);
Core\Debug::dump($nested);
Core\Debug::dump($vault);
Core\Debug::dump($ring);
Core\Debug::dump($ring);

// The runner reads an error expectation as "this run must fail"
// (`nvs_test::Case::expects_failure`), so a program that exits 0 has no way to
// state what it wrote to the diagnostic channel — and a dump writes nowhere
// else. This throw is what buys the `--EXPECTF-ERROR--` section below: the
// dumps are matched byte for byte and the fatal report they are followed by is
// absorbed by one `%A`. It is a property of the harness, not of `Core\Debug`.
throw new RuntimeError("the case ends here, so its standard error can be asserted");
--EXPECT--
subjects 7
ending in a newline 0
holding one at all 6
the ring twice yes
captured [echoed]
the capture holds the dump no
string(3) "a␍b"
array(2) [
  0 => int(10)
  1 => int(20)
]
array(1) [
  "k" => int(1)
]
array(1) [
  0 => array(1) [
    0 => int(7)
  ]
]
Vault#1 (2) {
  $token => [redacted]
  $n => int(1)
}
Ring#1 (2) {
  $next => [cycle -> #1]
  $name => string(1) "a"
}
Ring#1 (2) {
  $next => [cycle -> #1]
  $name => string(1) "a"
}
string(3) "a␍b"
array(2) [
  0 => int(10)
  1 => int(20)
]
array(1) [
  "k" => int(1)
]
array(1) [
  0 => array(1) [
    0 => int(7)
  ]
]
Vault#1 (2) {
  $token => [redacted]
  $n => int(1)
}
Ring#1 (2) {
  $next => [cycle -> #1]
  $name => string(1) "a"
}
Ring#1 (2) {
  $next => [cycle -> #1]
  $name => string(1) "a"
}
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
string(13) "not swallowed"
string(3) "a␍b"
array(2) [
  0 => int(10)
  1 => int(20)
]
array(1) [
  "k" => int(1)
]
array(1) [
  0 => array(1) [
    0 => int(7)
  ]
]
Vault#1 (2) {
  $token => [redacted]
  $n => int(1)
}
Ring#1 (2) {
  $next => [cycle -> #1]
  $name => string(1) "a"
}
Ring#1 (2) {
  $next => [cycle -> #1]
  $name => string(1) "a"
}
string(3) "a␍b"
array(2) [
  0 => int(10)
  1 => int(20)
]
array(1) [
  "k" => int(1)
]
array(1) [
  0 => array(1) [
    0 => int(7)
  ]
]
Vault#1 (2) {
  $token => [redacted]
  $n => int(1)
}
Ring#1 (2) {
  $next => [cycle -> #1]
  $name => string(1) "a"
}
Ring#1 (2) {
  $next => [cycle -> #1]
  $name => string(1) "a"
}
